---
name: object-apply-two
description: "two objects may each declare `apply`, and a hot `apply` may be JIT-compiled"
backends: [int, jvm, v2, js]
---

# Two objects may each declare `apply`

The interpreter's JIT emits, for every compiled function, a static method named after the ssc
function AND the functional-interface bridge `public T apply(…)`. When the ssc function is itself
called `apply`, those are one name with one signature:

```
method apply(long,long) is already defined in class GenJit_apply_…
apply(long,long) in GenJit_apply_… cannot implement apply(long,long) in LongFn2
```

**The two-object shape is how it was FOUND, not what causes it** — the cause is the name, so any
`apply` reaching the JIT collides. `object O { def apply(x) }` is plain Scala, so this is an
ordinary program, not an exotic one.

The `while` loop is here to make the last object's `apply` hot enough to be JIT-compiled: without
it this case would only exercise the interpreter's tree walk and could pass with the generator still
broken.

```scalascript
object O:
  def apply(x: Int): String = "u:" + x.toString

object Two:
  def apply(a: Int, b: Int): Int = a * 10 + b

def main() =
  println(O(7))
  println(Two(3, 4))

  var acc = 0
  var i = 0
  while i < 60000 do
    acc = acc + Two(i, 1)
    i = i + 1
  println(acc > 0)
```
